Durarara!! AU
Chapter 4 – Part 2
Curtis’s weight shifted forward—
—and the world cracked sideways.
A heavy wooden 2x4 slammed into the back of his head with a sickening CRACK. The impact was brutal, loud enough to cut through the street noise. Curtis staggered hard, one knee dropping toward the pavement as blood immediately began to drip from beneath his braids, dark against the white of his shirt collar.
Anri’s voice rose in sharp alarm.
Shizuo spun toward the new threat.
Izaya’s eyes widened with genuine surprise.
For a moment it looked like the tall transfer student might go down completely.
Instead, Curtis planted his hand against the ground, pushed himself back up to his full height, and stood there swaying only once before his balance returned. Blood ran in a thin line down the side of his neck. His expression, which had been building toward something dangerous, settled back into an eerie, controlled calm.
He reached into his pocket, pulled out a cigarette, and lit it with steady hands. The lighter clicked. Smoke curled upward as he took a slow drag, mismatched eyes half-lidded against the pain.
The street went quiet in a different way than before.
Anri stared at him, face pale. “Curtis-kun… you’re bleeding—”
Masaomi’s earlier satisfaction had vanished, replaced by real wariness. Mikado looked horrified. Even Shizuo frowned, thrown off by the fact that someone had just taken a full board to the skull and responded by lighting a smoke.
Izaya’s smile returned slowly, sharper than ever.
From the side street, laughter and footsteps approached.
Hiroshi had come back.
This time he wasn’t alone. A small group of guys followed him, cheap confidence restored by numbers. One of them—the one still holding the 2x4—grinned proudly at the hit he had landed. Hiroshi pointed at Curtis, the uneven patch of hair on his own head still obvious.
“Told you I’d be back, you tall freak,” Hiroshi called, voice full of borrowed bravery. “Dollars don’t take that kind of disrespect!”
Curtis exhaled a stream of smoke, blood still dripping steadily down his neck, and looked at the new arrivals with the same flat calm he had shown all day.
Curtis took one slow drag from the cigarette, blood still running down the side of his neck, and looked at the group with flat, cold eyes.
“I’m going to hang you all.”
He moved before anyone could react.
The punch was short, brutal, and obscenely strong. Curtis’s fist connected with the chest of the guy still holding the 2x4. The sound was a deep, ugly thud that seemed to push the air out of the street itself. The man left the ground completely. His body rocketed backward across the pavement, limbs flailing, and every piece of clothing on him—shirt, pants, shoes, even socks—ripped away from his body mid-flight as if the force itself had stripped him clean. He hit the far wall of the opposite building naked, upside-down, and crumpled into an unconscious heap.
The 2x4 clattered to the ground where he had been standing.
For a full second, nobody moved.
Anri’s hands had flown up to cover her mouth, eyes wide with pure shock behind her glasses.
Mikado made a strangled sound that might have been a scream if it had more air behind it.
Masaomi stared, mouth open, the earlier confrontation with Curtis suddenly recontextualized in the worst possible way.
Shizuo’s anger short-circuited into something closer to startled recognition—the kind of strength that made even him pause.
Izaya’s face lit up with unfiltered, ecstatic delight.
“Oh,” Izaya breathed, almost reverent. “Oh, I knew you were interesting.”
Hiroshi and the remaining friends stood frozen, the borrowed courage from numbers evaporating in real time as they stared at their naked, unconscious companion crumpled against the wall across the street. One of them actually took a step backward.
Curtis exhaled smoke through his nose, blood still dripping steadily from his head, and rolled his shoulder once as if he had simply thrown out a piece of trash.
Curtis exhaled another thin stream of smoke, blood still trailing down his neck, and looked at the remaining group with pure, flat contempt.
“Don’t stop now. There’s more of you than me.” A cold pause. “What’s wrong?”
They didn’t get the chance to answer.
Curtis moved.
What followed was less a fight and more a demolition. He blurred forward with the same unnatural speed and force that had cracked concrete the night before. The first two went down almost simultaneously—one from a short, brutal elbow that dropped him like a puppet with cut strings, the other from a palm strike that sent him skidding across the pavement on his back. A third tried to run and was caught by the collar, slammed face-first into a parked bicycle hard enough to wrap the metal around him.
Hiroshi was left for last.
The yellow-hoodied boy tried to backpedal, face white, hands raised in a panicked attempt at surrender that came far too late. Curtis closed the distance in two strides and drove a kick straight into his midsection. The impact launched Hiroshi clean off the ground, body folding around the force as he flew upward.
Curtis’s hand snapped out, caught him by the ankle at the peak of the arc, and slammed him back down onto the street with a sickening, final crack of pavement and bone. Hiroshi bounced once and stayed down, motionless.
The street went completely still except for the soft drip of blood from Curtis’s head and the faint curl of cigarette smoke.
Anri stood frozen, both hands still near her mouth, staring at the trail of broken bodies with something close to horror.
Mikado had gone sheet-white. “He… he just…”
Masaomi didn’t finish any thought. He simply stared, the earlier punch he had thrown at Curtis suddenly feeling like the single stupidest decision of his week.
Shizuo’s fists were still clenched, but the pure rage from earlier had been replaced by a sharp, assessing look. The kind he usually reserved for things that might actually be able to trade blows with him.
Izaya looked like he was witnessing a religious experience.
“Beautiful,” he whispered, eyes shining. “Absolutely beautiful.”
Curtis stood among the wreckage of the group, cigarette still burning between his fingers, long black braids hanging still, and calmly surveyed the result of less than ten seconds of work.
Curtis turned.
The cigarette was still burning between his fingers. Blood continued its slow drip down the side of his neck. The calm on his face had thinned into something sharper and far less patient.
“You both are pissing me off too.”
He moved on Shizuo first.
The punch was clean, heavy, and obscenely strong—the same force that had stripped a man of his clothes and put Hiroshi through the pavement. It caught Shizuo square in the chest. For the first time in a long time, the strongest man in Ikebukuro left his feet. Shizuo crashed backward into the street hard enough to crack the asphalt beneath him, skidding several meters before coming to a stop in a low cloud of dust.
Izaya barely had time to process the sight before Curtis was already on him.
The second punch landed with the same brutal efficiency. Izaya’s body folded around the impact and he was sent flying, slamming into a row of parked bikes that collapsed in a noisy, tangled heap around him. A pained, undignified wheeze escaped the information broker as he tried to remember how lungs worked.
The street went deathly quiet.
Anri stared, frozen.
Mikado made a small, strangled noise.
Masaomi’s face had gone blank with pure disbelief.
Shizuo pushed himself up onto one elbow, blood at the corner of his mouth, blond hair hanging in his eyes as he stared at the tall foreigner who had just put him on the ground. The usual mindless rage was tempered by something rarer—genuine recognition that the person in front of him could actually hit.
Izaya, half-buried in bicycles, let out a weak, breathless laugh that sounded more unhinged than amused.
Curtis stood in the middle of the wreckage, smoke curling from the cigarette still held loosely between his fingers, and looked at both of them with cold, mismatched eyes.
Shizuo was on his feet again in a blur of pure violence.
The counterpunch came with everything he had—raw, unrestricted strength that had turned street signs into weapons and sent grown men through walls. His fist crashed into Curtis’s face with a thunderous impact that echoed down the street.
Curtis’s head snapped sideways hard. Blood sprayed from his mouth and the already open wound on his head. His body was driven several steps back, boots scraping against the pavement, but he did not go down. He staggered, caught himself, and slowly straightened, spitting a mouthful of blood to the side as the cigarette fell from his fingers and died on the ground.
For a moment the only sound was both of them breathing.
Then the reactions hit.
Anri’s voice broke first. “Curtis-kun!” She looked genuinely frightened now, hands clenched tightly in front of her as she stared at the blood running freely down his face.
Mikado had both hands in his hair. “They’re going to kill each other—!”
Masaomi stood rigid, the earlier conflicts completely forgotten. “That foreigner’s still standing… after a full hit from Shizuo.”
Izaya, still half-tangled in the collapsed bikes, stared with wide, gleaming eyes and an almost reverent grin.
“Yes… yes, this is perfect. Two monsters. Right here in the open.”
Shizuo flexed his punching hand, eyes locked on Curtis with a new, sharper intensity. The usual mindless fury was still there, but underneath it was the rare recognition that he had just hit something that could take it and remain standing.
Curtis wiped the blood from his mouth with the back of his hand, long black braids hanging heavy with it, and met Shizuo’s glare with mismatched eyes that had gone completely cold.
Curtis spat another thin line of blood to the side and fixed Shizuo with a cold, mismatched stare.
“You. Throwing trash cans around and treating those as collateral.” His voice was rough from the blood but steady. “What’s wrong with you?”
Shizuo’s eye twitched hard.
“The hell did you just say to me?!” The blond man’s voice rose into a full snarl as he took a step forward, fists clenched so tightly the knuckles went white. “That flea was right there and you were standing next to him—don’t start crying about collateral when you decided to get involved!”
Izaya, still extracting himself from the pile of bikes, raised one hand weakly.
“In my defense, I never asked the foreigner to stand so close—”
“Shut up, Izaya!” both Curtis and Shizuo snapped at the same time.
Anri flinched at the combined volume. Mikado looked like he wanted to be anywhere else. Masaomi kept his distance, clearly calculating how fast the situation could still get worse.
Shizuo’s glare locked back onto Curtis, pure aggression rolling off him in waves.
“You hit me first, you damn freak. Don’t start lecturing me about how I fight when you’re the one who put me in the pavement!”
Curtis’s eyes narrowed, blood still running freely down the side of his face.
“Bullshit. You hit me with that trash can.”
He started walking forward.
Shizuo matched him step for step, pure aggression rolling off every movement. The two closed the distance until they stood nearly chest-to-chest in the middle of the ruined street. At 6’3”, Curtis had the height advantage—just enough that Shizuo had to tilt his head slightly to keep the glare locked. The two inches felt more significant with the way Curtis carried himself, long black braids hanging heavy with blood, mismatched eyes cold and unblinking.
Neither of them spoke for a moment.
The air between them was tight enough to cut.
Anri had gone completely still. Mikado looked like he was watching two natural disasters decide which one got to destroy the block first. Masaomi’s hand hovered near his pocket, clearly debating whether there was any point in trying to stop what was coming. Izaya, for once, stayed quiet—eyes shining with pure anticipation as the two strongest forces currently in the street measured each other at point-blank range.
Shizuo’s fists flexed at his sides.
Curtis’s breathing stayed heavy and controlled, the earlier fury still simmering under the surface.
One more word—or one more movement—and the street was going to break.
Curtis didn’t step back.
Standing nearly chest-to-chest with Shizuo, blood still dripping steadily from his head, he asked the question in a low, even voice:
“What are we going to do about this?”
The words hung between them, quiet but heavy.
Shizuo’s eye twitched. For a second it looked like pure violence was about to answer for him. His fists stayed clenched at his sides, the air around him practically vibrating with the urge to swing again.
“…You hit me,” Shizuo growled. “I hit you. You still standing. I’m still standing.” His voice was rough, strained with the effort of not immediately throwing another punch. “You got a real damn way of pissing people off, you know that?”
Izaya, from his safer distance, couldn’t help himself.
“This is the part where one of you decides the street isn’t big enough for both of you,” he offered helpfully. “I vote for more property damage.”
“Shut up, Izaya!” Shizuo barked without looking away from Curtis.
Anri’s voice came quieter, almost lost under the tension.
“…Curtis-kun. Please.”
Mikado looked like he was one loud noise away from bolting. Masaomi kept his eyes locked on both of them, waiting to see which way the standoff would break.
Curtis and Shizuo remained locked in place, two inches of height and a whole lot of unresolved force standing between any kind of peaceful ending.
Curtis’s eyes shifted.
He looked past Shizuo’s shoulder toward Anri for a brief second—taking in the worry on her face, the way she had said his name—before turning his mismatched gaze back to the blond man standing nearly chest-to-chest with him.
“You really didn’t mean to hit me with the trash can?”
The question came quieter than the previous exchanges, but it still carried weight. It was the first real opening either of them had given that wasn’t pure aggression.
Shizuo’s jaw worked. The fury in his expression didn’t vanish, but it flickered—interrupted by the unexpected directness of the question. He hated explanations almost as much as he hated Izaya, and being asked to clarify his intent while still locked in a standoff clearly grated on him.
“…I was aiming for the flea,” Shizuo muttered, voice still rough. “You were just standing too damn close to him. I don’t throw shit at random people for fun.”
He glared harder, as if resenting that he had answered at all.
“Doesn’t mean I’m not still pissed you punched me.”
Behind them, Izaya raised a finger.
“Technically I was the intended target, so—”
“One more word and I’m finishing what the trash can started,” Shizuo snapped without looking at him.
Anri watched the exchange closely, hope and tension mixed in her expression. Mikado and Masaomi remained silent, waiting to see if the temperature would actually drop or if this was only a brief pause before the street got destroyed.
Curtis exhaled once through his nose, then stepped away from Shizuo without another word.
He walked straight over to where Izaya was still extracting himself from the collapsed bikes, reached down, and grabbed the information broker by the front of his coat collar. Izaya made a sharp, undignified sound of protest as he was hauled upright with almost no effort.
Curtis turned back toward Shizuo, still holding Izaya like a particularly annoying piece of luggage, and gave the blond man a flat look.
“Here.”
He gave Izaya a slight shake for emphasis, then met Shizuo’s eyes directly.
“We good?”
The street held its breath again.
Izaya’s eyes widened in pure offense and rising panic.
“Excuse me—?! I am not a peace offering—put me down, you absolute—”
Shizuo stared at the scene in front of him: the bloodied foreigner casually holding the flea out by the collar like he was returning a borrowed item. For a second the pure rage in his expression short-circuited into something closer to baffled irritation.
Anri looked torn between relief and secondhand embarrassment. Mikado’s mouth was open. Masaomi pinched the bridge of his nose like he was developing a migraine in real time.
Shizuo’s fists remained clenched, but the immediate urge to destroy the entire block had clearly been interrupted by the sheer absurdity of the gesture.
Izaya’s hand moved faster than his words.
There was a sharp, metallic click, and then the switchblade was already buried in Curtis’s shoulder—once, then a second time in a quick, vicious follow-up. The information broker’s expression was a mix of panicked self-preservation and the same old malicious glee he always wore when he managed to turn a situation even uglier.
Curtis’s grip on Izaya’s collar tightened hard enough to make the fabric strain.
“You little rat—!”
The words came out low and furious, mismatched eyes flashing with fresh anger as blood began to spread across the white of his shirt from the two new wounds.
Shizuo’s reaction was instantaneous and nuclear.
“IZAYA, YOU SON OF A BITCH!”
The blond man’s earlier hesitation vanished completely. Whatever fragile pause the standoff had reached was obliterated in a single breath of pure, redirected rage. Shizuo launched forward with murder written across every line of his body, completely focused on the flea who had just stabbed someone while being used as a peace offering.
Anri cried out in alarm. Mikado stumbled backward. Masaomi swore under his breath and looked like he was seriously considering whether this street was about to become a crime scene in the next ten seconds.
Curtis still had Izaya by the collar, blood running freely from both the head wound and the fresh stab wounds in his shoulder, and for the first time all day his expression promised something far worse than a simple punch.
Izaya didn’t wait for the consequences.
The moment Curtis’s attention flickered toward the pain in his shoulder, the information broker twisted free with the practiced desperation of someone who had escaped Shizuo more times than he could count. He hit the ground running, coat flapping behind him as he bolted down the nearest side street and vanished into the maze of Ikebukuro before either of the two stronger men could grab him again.
“Ikeaaaa—!” Shizuo’s enraged roar followed him, but the flea was already gone.
Curtis remained where he stood.
Blood ran freely from the two stab wounds in his shoulder, adding fresh stains to the ones already soaking the white shirt from his head. He rolled the injured shoulder once, tested the range of motion, and looked more annoyed than actually hurt. The earlier fury cooled back into irritation as he glanced down at himself.
He muttered under his breath, voice flat and disgusted:
“…I don’t got many uniform pants or many white shirts. Shit.”
The sheer practicality of the complaint after everything that had just happened left a short, stunned silence in its wake.
Anri stared at him, caught between residual fear and complete disbelief that clothing was his primary concern while blood dripped steadily down his arm.
Mikado looked like his brain had simply stopped processing.
Masaomi let out a long, exhausted breath and dragged a hand down his face.
“This guy…” he muttered. “This guy is not normal.”
Shizuo, still bristling with leftover rage at Izaya’s escape, turned his glare back toward Curtis—only to pause at the sight of the bloodied foreigner more bothered by his ruined shirt than by two stab wounds and a head injury.
Curtis simply stood there in the middle of the wreckage, long black braids heavy with blood, white shirt ruined, and looked like the biggest inconvenience of the entire afternoon was going to be finding clean clothes.
Shizuo turned fully toward him, still simmering but no longer purely murderous.
“Hey! You’re stabbed!” He jabbed a finger at the blood soaking through Curtis’s white shirt. “You’re coming with me.”
Curtis glanced down at the two wounds in his shoulder like they were a minor inconvenience, then back up at Shizuo with complete seriousness.
“Why? I can close this with some gauze and duct tape. Maybe some glue too.”
The response was so flat and practical that it briefly short-circuited the remaining tension in the street.
Anri’s concern immediately sharpened. “Curtis-kun, that’s not—those are stab wounds. You need actual medical attention.”
Mikado looked personally offended by the suggestion. “Duct tape?! You can’t just duct-tape stab wounds!”
Masaomi stared at Curtis like he was evaluating a new species. “You’re serious. You’re actually serious right now.”
Shizuo’s expression twisted between lingering anger and pure disbelief.
“The hell is wrong with you?!” he snapped. “You’re bleeding all over the damn street and talking about hardware supplies! You’re coming with me whether you like it or not—Shinra can at least stop you from dropping dead like an idiot.”
Curtis raised an eyebrow, blood still dripping steadily from both his head and shoulder, and looked genuinely unconvinced that a doctor was necessary.
Shizuo didn’t give him a choice.
He grabbed Curtis by the uninjured arm with the kind of grip that made arguing pointless and started hauling him down the street. Curtis clicked his tongue in mild irritation but didn’t resist beyond a token pull, long black braids swaying as he was forced to match the blond man’s pace.
As they passed Anri, Curtis glanced back over his shoulder, blood still dripping steadily from his head and the two stab wounds.
“Sorry, Anri. Can’t walk you home.” His tone was almost casual despite the state of him. “Apparently I’m going to get stitches. See you all tomorrow at school.”
Anri opened her mouth, then closed it again, clearly still processing the entire afternoon. She managed a small, uncertain nod.
Mikado watched them go with a dazed expression. Masaomi just shook his head, muttering something under his breath about foreigners and monsters. The wreckage of the earlier fight—scattered hair, broken knife, unconscious bodies, and a naked man against the far wall—remained as evidence of how quickly the street had turned into a disaster zone.
Shizuo kept a firm hold on Curtis’s arm as they disappeared down the block, already barking about how Shinra was going to have a field day with the amount of blood the tall idiot was leaving on the pavement.
The two of them walked off together, one furious and the other mostly annoyed about his ruined shirt, leaving the rest of Ikebukuro to deal with the aftermath.
The remaining group had barely started to process the two bloodied figures walking away when a massive shadow fell over the street.
Simon stepped into view, towering over everyone present as usual, a stack of Russia Sushi flyers still tucked under one arm. He took in the wreckage—the unconscious bodies, the scattered hair, the broken knife, the general atmosphere of recent violence—and nodded solemnly.
“Good,” he said in his thick, earnest accent. “Shizuo found foreigner to be good friend. Fight always bad.” His dark eyes swept over the evidence of Izaya’s involvement with clear disapproval. “Izaya is wrong for stabbing kind foreigner.”
The sheer simplicity of the statement after everything that had just happened left a short silence.
Masaomi stared up at the giant. “…Simon. How do you even know what happened?”
Simon tapped the side of his head with one massive finger, as if the answer were obvious.
“People talk. City small when important things happen. Tall foreigner with braids strong. Shizuo strong. Together maybe less lonely.” He nodded again, satisfied with his own conclusion. “Izaya still wrong.”
Anri looked between the departing path Shizuo and Curtis had taken and the enormous Russian now offering moral judgment over the aftermath. Mikado simply looked overwhelmed by how many larger-than-life people had been involved in a single afternoon.
Simon held out a flyer toward them with a hopeful expression.
“You come Russia Sushi later. Eat. Feel better. Fighting make empty stomach.”
